## Why This Is Asked
This question evaluates your ability to design distributed workflows across microservices with CQRS/ES, ensuring tenant isolation, idempotency, and reliable message delivery.

## Key Concepts
- Long-running sagas with compensating transactions for rollback scenarios
- CQRS and Event Sourcing with versioned events for auditability and replay
- Outbox pattern ensuring at-least-once delivery
- Idempotency via correlationId and deduplication checks
- Per-tenant read models maintaining data isolation

## Code Example
```javascript
// Event-to-command mapping with deduplication
function handleEvent(evt, state) {
  if (state.processed?.includes(evt.id)) return;

  const cmd = mapEventToCommand(evt);
  enqueueToOutbox(cmd);
  state.processed = [...(state.processed || []), evt.id];
}

// Saga orchestration with compensation
async function executeOnboardSaga(tenantId, correlationId) {
  try {
    await TenantAuth.create(tenantId, correlationId);
    await Billing.setupQuota(tenantId, correlationId);
    await DataIngestion.provision(tenantId, correlationId);
    await Analytics.initialize(tenantId, correlationId);
  } catch (error) {
    await compensateSaga(tenantId, correlationId);
  }
}

async function compensateSaga(tenantId, correlationId) {
  await Analytics.deprovision(tenantId, correlationId);
  await DataIngestion.stop(tenantId, correlationId);
  await Billing.revertQuota(tenantId, correlationId);
  await TenantAuth.delete(tenantId, correlationId);
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests design of robust, multi-tenant cache invalidation with Redis and Memcached, covering versioning, streams, stampede protection, and testability.

## Key Concepts
- Versioned per-tenant keys
- Redis Streams or Pub/Sub for invalidation
- Stampede protection via short TTLs and mutexes
- Cross-service invalidation consistency
- Observability and test plans

## Code Example
```javascript
function publishInvalidation(tenantId, itemId, ver){
  redis.xadd(`cache_invalidate:tenant:${tenantId}`, '*', 'item', itemId, 'ver', ver);
}
async function readItem(tenantId, itemId){
  const v = await redis.get(`tenant:${tenantId}:item:${itemId}:v`);
  const cached = await redis.get(`tenant:${tenantId}:item:${itemId}:data`);
  if (cached && v === latestVersionFromStore(itemId)){
    return JSON.parse(cached);
  } else {
    const fresh = await fetchFromStore(tenantId, itemId);
    await redis.set(`tenant:${tenantId}:item:${itemId}:data`, JSON.stringify(fresh));
    await redis.set(`tenant:${tenantId}:item:${itemId}:v`, latestVersionFromStore(itemId));
    return fresh;
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would this adapt to Memcached-only environments?
- How would you measure correctness and latency impact?
- How would you handle cascading invalidations across services?

## Why Asked
Tests mastery of Spring's IoC container implementation and enterprise architecture patterns for scalable backend systems.

## Key Concepts
- Inversion of Control (IoC) Container
- ApplicationContext lifecycle management
- Bean post-processors and dependency wiring
- Constructor injection vs field vs setter injection
- Dependency resolution and proxy generation
- Singleton vs prototype scope
- @ComponentScan and autowiring strategies
- Circular dependency resolution

## Code Example
```
@Service
@Transactional
public class UserService {
    private final UserRepository userRepository;
    private final CacheManager cacheManager;

    @Autowired
    public UserService(UserRepository userRepository, 
                       @Qualifier("redisCacheManager") CacheManager cacheManager) {
        this.userRepository = userRepository;
        this.cacheManager = cacheManager;
    }

    @PostConstruct
    public void init() {
        // Bean initialization logic
    }
}

@Configuration
@EnableTransactionManagement
@ComponentScan(basePackages = "com.example")
public class AppConfig {
    @Bean
    @Primary
    public UserRepository userRepository(DataSource dataSource) {
        return new JpaUserRepository(dataSource);
    }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How does Spring resolve circular dependencies?
- What's the difference between @Autowired and @Inject?
- How do you implement custom bean post-processors?
- What are the implications of different bean scopes in distributed systems?

## Why This Is Asked

This question evaluates practical cache invalidation strategies in multi-region Redis deployments, testing understanding of versioned cache keys, cross-region synchronization, and TTL management. It also assesses knowledge of Redis pub/sub versus keyspace notifications and write-through caching trade-offs.

## Key Concepts

- Versioned cache keys with TTL management
- Cross-region invalidation patterns
- Redis pub/sub vs keyspace notifications
- Atomic operations and consistency guarantees
- Write-through vs write-behind caching strategies

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudo: bumpVersion(id)
const newVer = await redis.incr(`product:${id}:ver`);
await redis.publish('cache:invalidate', JSON.stringify({id, ver: newVer}));
```

```javascript
// Pseudo: getCache(id)
const currentVer = await redis.get(`product:${id}:ver`);
const cached = await redis.get(`product:${id}:v${currentVer}`);
if (!cached) {
  // Cache miss - refresh from source
  const data = await fetchFromDatabase(id);
  await redis.setex(`product:${id}:v${currentVer}`, 120, JSON.stringify(data));
  return data;
}
return JSON.parse(cached);
```
